## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
<h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Exploring My Community – Lesson # 4</h1>
<p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Exploring My Community</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Market!</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write simple phrases or sentences about personal experiences and familiar items with support and guidance. Interactive: Contribute a word to a class story or sentence, with help.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple sentences about visiting community places with support (e.g., "I go to the market with my mom."). Can contribute a word or phrase to a class project about community places with support (e.g., adding "park" to a class story about the neighborhood). Can write basic words or simple sentences about community places (e.g., "park," school," and "library").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</i></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of community places previously learned (park, market, school, library, church, store). Students identify each place chorally. The teacher then quickly writes the name of each place under its flashcard on the board. Students participate in a quick "Match the Word" game: the teacher calls out a word (e.g., "market"), and students point to the corresponding written word and picture.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to the "market" flashcard and the word "market." The teacher says, "This is a market. I go to the market." The teacher writes the simple sentence "I go to the market." on the board, pointing to each word as it is written. Then the teacher points to the "park" flashcard, says "The park is big." and writes "The park is big." on the board. The teacher explicitly models forming simple sentences with known vocabulary.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'market' a place?" (Expected answer: Yes!)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can we go to the market?" (Expected answer: Yes!)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the park small or big?" (Expected answer: Big!)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Exploring My Community"</i></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An illustrated short narrative (e.g., "My Trip to the Market") projected on the board or printed as a handout. The narrative uses simple sentences and known vocabulary words related to community places, verbs (go, see, meet), and adjectives (big, busy).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Narrative:</i></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"Hello! My name is Maya. This is my mom. We go to the market. The market is big and busy. We see many fruits and vegetables. We meet a friendly neighbor. I like the market!"</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to the words and pictures. Students listen and follow along.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing places and activities: "The park is big. I play there." Answering questions: "Where is the library?" "Next to the market."</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> The teacher asks simple questions about the story: "Who goes to the market?" (Maya and her mom). "What place is the story about?" (The market).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Show a card):</b> Students show a "True" or "False" card (or thumbs up/down).
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Maya goes to the park." (False)</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The market is big." (True)</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"They see vegetables." (True)</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Accuracy focus on Writing</i></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Present simple (e.g., "It's the market."), Adjectives + nouns (e.g., "big park").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> park, market, school, library, church, store, go, see, big, small, busy, friendly.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Teacher models /p/ in "park" and /m/ in "market" when guiding students to write.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
        <ol>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Word/Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with pictures of community places (e.g., market, park, school, church). Below each picture, there are incomplete sentences with a blank space. Students look at the picture and complete the sentence by writing the missing word(s). A word bank (market, park, school, church, big, busy) is provided on the board or worksheet for support.
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: Picture of a market: "It's a _________." (market)</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: Picture of a park: "The park is _________." (big)</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example: Picture of a church: "I see a _________." (church)</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Sentence Building with Options:</b> The teacher writes sentence starters on the board or provides a handout. Students choose words from two columns to create a simple sentence and write it down.
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Column A (Subject/Verb): "I go to the...", "The _______ is..."</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Column B (Places/Adjectives): "market", "park", "school", "big", "busy", "friendly"</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students might write: "I go to the school." or "The market is busy."</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Peer Sentence Check:</b> Students work in pairs with their completed sentences from the previous activity. One student reads a sentence they wrote, and the other student checks if the words are correct and makes sense with the target vocabulary. They help each other with spelling for basic words. The teacher circulates, provides guidance, and models corrections.</li>
        </ol>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Exploring My Community"</i></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided individual sentence writing to describe a favorite community place.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a large picture of a simple community map with several labeled places (park, market, school, library, church).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students choose one place from the map that they like to visit or want to talk about.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">On a blank paper, students draw a picture of their chosen community place.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below their drawing, students write 2-3 simple sentences about the place, using sentence starters and vocabulary provided on the board as support.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sentence Starters on board:</b>
            <ul>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This is a _________."</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I go to the _________."</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It is _________." (e.g., big, busy, friendly)</li>
              <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I see _________."</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, offering help with vocabulary and sentence structure, encouraging students to use words like 'market', 'park', 'big', 'busy', 'go', 'see'.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in writing simple sentences about community places, using target vocabulary, and completing the guided writing task.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Formative assessment strategy</i></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the writing activity, focusing on participation and effort. A quick review of submitted written sentences for basic understanding and target word use.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their drawings and the 2-3 simple sentences describing their chosen community place.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writes at least 1-2 simple sentences about a community place.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least 2 target vocabulary words (e.g., market, park, school, big, go, see).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sentences are mostly understandable with support.</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick self-reflection by circling an answer or showing thumbs up/down.</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write about a place in my community." (Yes / Almost / No)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I used words like 'market' or 'park' in my writing." (Yes / No)</li>
        </ul>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Student and Teacher Reflection</i></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> The teacher asks the following questions and encourages a few students to share their answers:</p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to write today?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult for you to write today?"</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of writing simple sentences did students grasp well? (e.g., using "I go to the...")</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What areas needed more support? (e.g., spelling specific vocabulary, using adjectives correctly).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds might be needed for similar writing tasks in the future? (e.g., more visual prompts, more sentence frames, small group work for writing).</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Mediation & Integration), students will use the sentences they wrote today to contribute to a class project, such as a collaborative "Our Community Book" or a large "Community Map" display, reinforcing their understanding and promoting shared learning.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>